window.__programmeIndexChunk("contexts",{"824c102e6522fae14120ea06613a398da835332e1dfda52e5a95eccb84cc936f":{"markdown":"Begin with any increasing positive net \\(0\\leq x_i\\leq C1\\) in \\(M\\). For \\(j\\geq i\\), the positive difference is between zero and \\(C1\\). Continuous calculus gives \\((x_j-x_i)^2\\leq C(x_j-x_i)\\), hence\n\n<a id=\"equation-co1\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\|(x_j-x_i)\\xi\\|^2\\leq C\\langle(x_j-x_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\tag{CO1}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(\\xi\\), the quadratic values have a finite scalar supremum. Once they are close to it, comparison with a common upper index makes both vector differences small by CO1 and the triangle inequality. Thus \\(x_i\\xi\\) is a Cauchy net. To obtain a limit from Hilbert completeness, choose increasing indices after which its errors are below \\(2^{-n}\\); their sequence converges, and the same errors make the entire net converge to that limit. The vector limits define a linear bounded positive operator of norm at most \\(C\\). Strong convergence puts it in the weakly closed algebra \\(M\\). Passing each quadratic order inequality to the limit shows that this operator dominates the net and is below every selfadjoint upper bound. It is its supremum. Adding a scalar reduces uniformly bounded increasing selfadjoint nets to this case.\n\nIn an abelian von Neumann algebra \\(D\\), commuting selfadjoint elements have the pointwise maximum\n\n<a id=\"equation-co2\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\na\\vee b=\\tfrac12(a+b+|a-b|).\n\\tag{CO2}\n\\]\n\nCF6 identifies the commutative algebra generated by \\(a,b\\) with continuous scalar functions. If another selfadjoint element of \\(D\\) bounds both, it commutes with them and the same scalar comparison in the enlarged commutative algebra makes it bound their displayed maximum. This is a least upper bound in \\(D\\), not a lattice assertion for noncommuting operators.\n\nFor any uniformly bounded selfadjoint family in \\(D\\), finite maxima form a uniformly bounded increasing net. The preceding limit constructs its supremum in \\(D\\). Addition of a fixed selfadjoint element translates all upper bounds, so commutes with this supremum. A star automorphism is an order isomorphism; applying it and its inverse to the defining upper-bound inequalities proves that it carries a supremum to the supremum of the image family. This particular assertion uses order, without a continuity assumption on the map. Infima follow by changing signs.\n","html":"<p>Begin with any increasing positive net <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\leq x_i\\leq C1\\)\">\\(0\\leq x_i\\leq C1\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\geq i\\)\">\\(j\\geq i\\)<\/span>, the positive difference is between zero and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C1\\)\">\\(C1\\)<\/span>. Continuous calculus gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((x_j-x_i)^2\\leq C(x_j-x_i)\\)\">\\((x_j-x_i)^2\\leq C(x_j-x_i)\\)<\/span>, hence<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|(x_j-x_i)\\xi\\|^2\\leq C\\langle(x_j-x_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\tag{CO1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|(x_j-x_i)\\xi\\|^2\\leq C\\langle(x_j-x_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\tag{CO1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>, the quadratic values have a finite scalar supremum. Once they are close to it, comparison with a common upper index makes both vector differences small by CO1 and the triangle inequality. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_i\\xi\\)\">\\(x_i\\xi\\)<\/span> is a Cauchy net. To obtain a limit from Hilbert completeness, choose increasing indices after which its errors are below <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^{-n}\\)\">\\(2^{-n}\\)<\/span>; their sequence converges, and the same errors make the entire net converge to that limit. The vector limits define a linear bounded positive operator of norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. Strong convergence puts it in the weakly closed algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Passing each quadratic order inequality to the limit shows that this operator dominates the net and is below every selfadjoint upper bound. It is its supremum. Adding a scalar reduces uniformly bounded increasing selfadjoint nets to this case.<\/p>\n<p>In an abelian von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, commuting selfadjoint elements have the pointwise maximum<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\na\\vee b=\\tfrac12(a+b+|a-b|).\n\\tag{CO2}\n\\]\">\\[\na\\vee b=\\tfrac12(a+b+|a-b|).\n\\tag{CO2}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>CF6 identifies the commutative algebra generated by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a,b\\)\">\\(a,b\\)<\/span> with continuous scalar functions. If another selfadjoint element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> bounds both, it commutes with them and the same scalar comparison in the enlarged commutative algebra makes it bound their displayed maximum. This is a least upper bound in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, not a lattice assertion for noncommuting operators.<\/p>\n<p>For any uniformly bounded selfadjoint family in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, finite maxima form a uniformly bounded increasing net. The preceding limit constructs its supremum in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>. Addition of a fixed selfadjoint element translates all upper bounds, so commutes with this supremum. A star automorphism is an order isomorphism; applying it and its inverse to the defining upper-bound inequalities proves that it carries a supremum to the supremum of the image family. This particular assertion uses order, without a continuity assumption on the map. Infima follow by changing signs.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L125.md","start_line":23,"end_line":45,"recorded_range_hash_matched":true},"824817436f7bd60b118c76e0db63c4905d70577db0026bdfa385d7735195a2df":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.msf.transport\"><\/a>\n## 5. A singleton relation proves the equivalence\n\n**Theorem 5.1.** In Section 4, the operator-section and canonical-unitary conventions are equivalent. Either implies that \\(\\theta\\) and \\(\\theta^{-1}\\) send every Borel pointwise bounded algebra section to a Borel section. The theorem holds on an arbitrary standard Borel parameter space, without a measure, and does not require factor fibres. With completed-measurable data on a standard sigma-finite measured base, the same equivalence holds modulo one null set and the canonical unitary field has a Borel representative on a conull Borel subset. The null-set statement makes no assertion of a valid canonical map at exceptional points where no isomorphism was supplied.\n\n**Proof.** [MC1](OA-FLOW-MC.md#oa-flow.mc.1) gives \\(W_x\\) individually, so \\(\\dim H_x=\\dim K_x\\) at every point satisfying (MSF7). [DF2](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DF.html#compact-orthonormal-frames-and-measurable-dimension) makes dimension measurable and supplies Borel orthonormal coordinates. Partition \\(X\\) into the countably many common dimension strata \\(d\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,\\infty\\}\\). On one stratum identify the two Hilbert fields with \\(E_d=\\mathbb C^d\\) or \\(\\ell^2\\). Only the Hilbert spaces have been trivialized; \\(M_x,N_x,P_x,Q_x\\) still vary with \\(x\\).\n\nThe group \\(\\mathcal U(E_d)\\) is Polish in the strong topology. A compatible complete metric tests both \\(u\\) and \\(u^*\\) on a countable orthonormal basis, with summable bounded weights. A Cauchy sequence has strong limits for both components; their products give two inverse isometries, hence a unitary and its adjoint. This topology is the usual strong unitary topology because strong convergence of unitaries to a unitary implies strong convergence of their adjoints. Separability follows from its embedding into the countable product of vector-coordinate spaces. The same facts cover finite dimension. The corresponding operator-and-adjoint metric on the contraction ball is complete: a Cauchy sequence has strong limits \\(a,b\\), its vector coefficients give \\(b=a^*\\), and both norms remain at most one. Finite matrix compressions with rational approximations give separability. Matrix coefficients determine Borel maps into this ball: norms of the basis-vector differences are countable sums of squared coefficients, so preimages of its metric balls are Borel. Conversely evaluation and its coefficients are continuous. These are direct coordinate arguments and require no SVFF result.\n\nAssume the operator-section convention and write \\(d_j(x)=\\theta_x(b_j(x))\\). These are Borel contractions. In the fixed Hilbert coordinates define \\(\\mathscr R_d\\subseteq X_d\\times\\mathcal U(E_d)\\) by the following countably many tests:\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n &u b_j(x)u^*=d_j(x) &&(j\\geq1),\\\\\n &\\inf_k\\|u p_j(x)-q_k(x)\\|=0 &&(j\\geq1),\\\\\n &\\inf_j\\|u^*q_k(x)-p_j(x)\\|=0 &&(k\\geq1).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{MSF9}\n\\]\nThe first equality is tested on the countable orthonormal basis. The other two say \\(uP_x\\subseteq Q_x\\) and \\(u^*Q_x\\subseteq P_x\\), by density and closure. No generic selection of algebra isomorphisms occurs here; the specified images \\(d_j\\) are part of the input.\n\nThis relation is Borel. The action of a unitary on a varying Borel vector is jointly Borel, indeed continuous in its unitary/vector variables. Products of uniformly bounded strongly convergent operators are strongly continuous; applying this to operators and adjoints gives jointly continuous conjugation on the strong-star contraction ball. Thus each matrix equality and each norm in (MSF9) is Borel, and a countable infimum is Borel. The fibre relations are also closed in \\(u\\), although that extra fact is unnecessary for the following argument. Cone codes can equivalently be recognized through their dense sections, without describing the subset of the coding space consisting of all valid standard forms.\n\nAt every \\(x\\), the pointwise canonical unitary supplied by [MC1](OA-FLOW-MC.md#oa-flow.mc.1) belongs to \\(\\mathscr R_d\\), so the fibre is nonempty. Conversely suppose \\(u\\) passes (MSF9). For \\(a\\in(M_x)_1\\), choose a sequence from \\(b_j(x)\\) tending strong-star to \\(a\\). The pointwise canonical unitary \\(W_x\\), whose measurability has not yet been used, shows that \\(\\theta_x\\) is strong-star continuous on this bounded ball: it is conjugation by \\(W_x\\). Passing to the limit in the first tests therefore gives \\(uau^*=\\theta_x(a)\\). Scaling gives this on every \\(a\\in M_x\\). The two cone tests give \\(uP_x=Q_x\\). [MC1](OA-FLOW-MC.md#oa-flow.mc.1) uniqueness now forces \\(u=W_x\\), and also forces the conjugation identity in (MSF8). Hence \\(\\mathscr R_d\\) has exactly one point over each \\(x\\).\n\nThe projection \\(\\mathscr R_d\\to X_d\\) is a Borel bijection between standard Borel spaces: \\(\\mathscr R_d\\) is a Borel subset of the product of a standard Borel space and a Polish space. [PB Theorem 4.3(5)](../../foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/polish-spaces-and-standard-borel-spaces.html#oa-fnd-pb-06) makes its inverse Borel. Composing with the second projection proves that \\(x\\mapsto W_x\\) is Borel. The countable union over dimensions proves the intrinsic assertion on the original two Hilbert fields. This is the point where uniqueness yields everywhere Borelness, stronger than a general selection theorem.\n\nConversely, if \\(W_x\\) is Borel, [DF5](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DF.html#pointwise-bounded-operator-fields-adjoints-and-exact-norm-tests) makes \\(W_x\\), \\(W_x^*\\), and their products with any Borel operator section measurable; their coordinate expansions make them Borel in these coordinates. Consequently \\(a_x\\mapsto W_xa_xW_x^*\\) and \\(c_x\\mapsto W_x^*c_xW_x\\) send all Borel sections to Borel sections. This proves the operator-section convention and the two-sided assertion, including pointwise bounded sections with nonuniform norm. Their algebra norms are preserved exactly, so essentially bounded sections are preserved with the same essential bound.\n\nFor completeness the countable operator tests alone also extend to every Borel contraction section without using the just-proved Borelness of \\(W\\). Let \\(\\delta_x\\) be the strong-star metric supplied by the Borel frame. For a Borel contraction \\(a_x\\), let \\(j_n(x)\\) be the least index satisfying \\(\\delta_x(b_{j_n(x)}(x),a_x)<1/n\\). Its level sets are Borel, so \\(d_{j_n(x)}(x)\\) is a Borel pasted contraction field. Pointwise bounded strong-star continuity of \\(\\theta_x\\), already justified by the individually existing [MC1](OA-FLOW-MC.md#oa-flow.mc.1) unitary, shows that it tends to \\(\\theta_x(a_x)\\). Countable coordinate limits prove Borelness. Divide any nonuniform pointwise bounded \\(a_x\\) by \\(1+\\|a_x\\|\\), a Borel scalar by [DF5](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DF.html#pointwise-bounded-operator-fields-adjoints-and-exact-norm-tests), and multiply back after applying the contraction conclusion. Thus the section convention is independent of the chosen dense family.\n\nFor the completed-measurable version, choose Borel versions of the countably many Hilbert frames, operator tests, conjugations and cone sections, and remove a common Borel null set containing their exceptions and any exceptions to the supplied isomorphism hypothesis. Polish-valued Borel versions exist by [SCF5](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-SCF.html#choose-a-witness-after-completing-the-measure)'s last step, or by the scalar coordinate version proof; countability is essential. On this conull Borel subset the Borel argument applies verbatim. Alternatively apply [SCF5](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-SCF.html#choose-a-witness-after-completing-the-measure) directly to the already coded (MSF9): it selects its unique canonical value in the completed measure, then gives a Borel representative almost everywhere. This alternative proves the measured assertion, not an everywhere Borel selector for arbitrary nonunique relations. The arbitrary standard Borel parameter theorem above cannot be strengthened by intersecting uncountably many parameter-dependent conull sets. \\(\\square\\)\n\n","html":"<h2>5. A singleton relation proves the equivalence<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Theorem 5.1.<\/strong> In Section 4, the operator-section and canonical-unitary conventions are equivalent. Either implies that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\theta^{-1}\\)<\/span> send every Borel pointwise bounded algebra section to a Borel section. The theorem holds on an arbitrary standard Borel parameter space, without a measure, and does not require factor fibres. With completed-measurable data on a standard sigma-finite measured base, the same equivalence holds modulo one null set and the canonical unitary field has a Borel representative on a conull Borel subset. The null-set statement makes no assertion of a valid canonical map at exceptional points where no isomorphism was supplied.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MC.html#oa-flow.mc.1\">MC1<\/a> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_x\\)\">\\(W_x\\)<\/span> individually, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\dim H_x=\\dim K_x\\)\">\\(\\dim H_x=\\dim K_x\\)<\/span> at every point satisfying (MSF7). <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DF.html#compact-orthonormal-frames-and-measurable-dimension\">DF2<\/a> makes dimension measurable and supplies Borel orthonormal coordinates. Partition <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> into the countably many common dimension strata <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,\\infty\\}\\)\">\\(d\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,\\infty\\}\\)<\/span>. On one stratum identify the two Hilbert fields with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_d=\\mathbb C^d\\)\">\\(E_d=\\mathbb C^d\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2\\)\">\\(\\ell^2\\)<\/span>. Only the Hilbert spaces have been trivialized; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_x,N_x,P_x,Q_x\\)\">\\(M_x,N_x,P_x,Q_x\\)<\/span> still vary with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The group <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal U(E_d)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal U(E_d)\\)<\/span> is Polish in the strong topology. A compatible complete metric tests both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^*\\)\">\\(u^*\\)<\/span> on a countable orthonormal basis, with summable bounded weights. A Cauchy sequence has strong limits for both components; their products give two inverse isometries, hence a unitary and its adjoint. This topology is the usual strong unitary topology because strong convergence of unitaries to a unitary implies strong convergence of their adjoints. Separability follows from its embedding into the countable product of vector-coordinate spaces. The same facts cover finite dimension. The corresponding operator-and-adjoint metric on the contraction ball is complete: a Cauchy sequence has strong limits <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a,b\\)\">\\(a,b\\)<\/span>, its vector coefficients give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=a^*\\)\">\\(b=a^*\\)<\/span>, and both norms remain at most one. Finite matrix compressions with rational approximations give separability. Matrix coefficients determine Borel maps into this ball: norms of the basis-vector differences are countable sums of squared coefficients, so preimages of its metric balls are Borel. Conversely evaluation and its coefficients are continuous. These are direct coordinate arguments and require no SVFF result.<\/p>\n<p>Assume the operator-section convention and write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_j(x)=\\theta_x(b_j(x))\\)\">\\(d_j(x)=\\theta_x(b_j(x))\\)<\/span>. These are Borel contractions. In the fixed Hilbert coordinates define <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr R_d\\subseteq X_d\\times\\mathcal U(E_d)\\)\">\\(\\mathscr R_d\\subseteq X_d\\times\\mathcal U(E_d)\\)<\/span> by the following countably many tests:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n &amp;u b_j(x)u^*=d_j(x) &amp;&amp;(j\\geq1),\\\\\n &amp;\\inf_k\\|u p_j(x)-q_k(x)\\|=0 &amp;&amp;(j\\geq1),\\\\\n &amp;\\inf_j\\|u^*q_k(x)-p_j(x)\\|=0 &amp;&amp;(k\\geq1).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{MSF9}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n &amp;u b_j(x)u^*=d_j(x) &amp;&amp;(j\\geq1),\\\\\n &amp;\\inf_k\\|u p_j(x)-q_k(x)\\|=0 &amp;&amp;(j\\geq1),\\\\\n &amp;\\inf_j\\|u^*q_k(x)-p_j(x)\\|=0 &amp;&amp;(k\\geq1).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{MSF9}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe first equality is tested on the countable orthonormal basis. The other two say <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(uP_x\\subseteq Q_x\\)\">\\(uP_x\\subseteq Q_x\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^*Q_x\\subseteq P_x\\)\">\\(u^*Q_x\\subseteq P_x\\)<\/span>, by density and closure. No generic selection of algebra isomorphisms occurs here; the specified images <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_j\\)\">\\(d_j\\)<\/span> are part of the input.<\/p>\n<p>This relation is Borel. The action of a unitary on a varying Borel vector is jointly Borel, indeed continuous in its unitary/vector variables. Products of uniformly bounded strongly convergent operators are strongly continuous; applying this to operators and adjoints gives jointly continuous conjugation on the strong-star contraction ball. Thus each matrix equality and each norm in (MSF9) is Borel, and a countable infimum is Borel. The fibre relations are also closed in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span>, although that extra fact is unnecessary for the following argument. Cone codes can equivalently be recognized through their dense sections, without describing the subset of the coding space consisting of all valid standard forms.<\/p>\n<p>At every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, the pointwise canonical unitary supplied by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MC.html#oa-flow.mc.1\">MC1<\/a> belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr R_d\\)\">\\(\\mathscr R_d\\)<\/span>, so the fibre is nonempty. Conversely suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> passes (MSF9). For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in(M_x)_1\\)\">\\(a\\in(M_x)_1\\)<\/span>, choose a sequence from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_j(x)\\)\">\\(b_j(x)\\)<\/span> tending strong-star to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>. The pointwise canonical unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_x\\)\">\\(W_x\\)<\/span>, whose measurability has not yet been used, shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_x\\)\">\\(\\theta_x\\)<\/span> is strong-star continuous on this bounded ball: it is conjugation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_x\\)\">\\(W_x\\)<\/span>. Passing to the limit in the first tests therefore gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(uau^*=\\theta_x(a)\\)\">\\(uau^*=\\theta_x(a)\\)<\/span>. Scaling gives this on every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M_x\\)\">\\(a\\in M_x\\)<\/span>. The two cone tests give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(uP_x=Q_x\\)\">\\(uP_x=Q_x\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MC.html#oa-flow.mc.1\">MC1<\/a> uniqueness now forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u=W_x\\)\">\\(u=W_x\\)<\/span>, and also forces the conjugation identity in (MSF8). Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr R_d\\)\">\\(\\mathscr R_d\\)<\/span> has exactly one point over each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr R_d\\to X_d\\)\">\\(\\mathscr R_d\\to X_d\\)<\/span> is a Borel bijection between standard Borel spaces: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr R_d\\)\">\\(\\mathscr R_d\\)<\/span> is a Borel subset of the product of a standard Borel space and a Polish space. <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/polish-spaces-and-standard-borel-spaces.html#oa-fnd-pb-06\">PB Theorem 4.3(5)<\/a> makes its inverse Borel. Composing with the second projection proves that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\mapsto W_x\\)\">\\(x\\mapsto W_x\\)<\/span> is Borel. The countable union over dimensions proves the intrinsic assertion on the original two Hilbert fields. This is the point where uniqueness yields everywhere Borelness, stronger than a general selection theorem.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_x\\)\">\\(W_x\\)<\/span> is Borel, <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DF.html#pointwise-bounded-operator-fields-adjoints-and-exact-norm-tests\">DF5<\/a> makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_x\\)\">\\(W_x\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_x^*\\)\">\\(W_x^*\\)<\/span>, and their products with any Borel operator section measurable; their coordinate expansions make them Borel in these coordinates. Consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_x\\mapsto W_xa_xW_x^*\\)\">\\(a_x\\mapsto W_xa_xW_x^*\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_x\\mapsto W_x^*c_xW_x\\)\">\\(c_x\\mapsto W_x^*c_xW_x\\)<\/span> send all Borel sections to Borel sections. This proves the operator-section convention and the two-sided assertion, including pointwise bounded sections with nonuniform norm. Their algebra norms are preserved exactly, so essentially bounded sections are preserved with the same essential bound.<\/p>\n<p>For completeness the countable operator tests alone also extend to every Borel contraction section without using the just-proved Borelness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_x\\)\">\\(\\delta_x\\)<\/span> be the strong-star metric supplied by the Borel frame. For a Borel contraction <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_x\\)\">\\(a_x\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j_n(x)\\)\">\\(j_n(x)\\)<\/span> be the least index satisfying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_x(b_{j_n(x)}(x),a_x)&lt;1/n\\)\">\\(\\delta_x(b_{j_n(x)}(x),a_x)&lt;1/n\\)<\/span>. Its level sets are Borel, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_{j_n(x)}(x)\\)\">\\(d_{j_n(x)}(x)\\)<\/span> is a Borel pasted contraction field. Pointwise bounded strong-star continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_x\\)\">\\(\\theta_x\\)<\/span>, already justified by the individually existing <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MC.html#oa-flow.mc.1\">MC1<\/a> unitary, shows that it tends to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_x(a_x)\\)\">\\(\\theta_x(a_x)\\)<\/span>. Countable coordinate limits prove Borelness. Divide any nonuniform pointwise bounded <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_x\\)\">\\(a_x\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1+\\|a_x\\|\\)\">\\(1+\\|a_x\\|\\)<\/span>, a Borel scalar by <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DF.html#pointwise-bounded-operator-fields-adjoints-and-exact-norm-tests\">DF5<\/a>, and multiply back after applying the contraction conclusion. Thus the section convention is independent of the chosen dense family.<\/p>\n<p>For the completed-measurable version, choose Borel versions of the countably many Hilbert frames, operator tests, conjugations and cone sections, and remove a common Borel null set containing their exceptions and any exceptions to the supplied isomorphism hypothesis. Polish-valued Borel versions exist by <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-SCF.html#choose-a-witness-after-completing-the-measure\">SCF5<\/a>'s last step, or by the scalar coordinate version proof; countability is essential. On this conull Borel subset the Borel argument applies verbatim. Alternatively apply <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-SCF.html#choose-a-witness-after-completing-the-measure\">SCF5<\/a> directly to the already coded (MSF9): it selects its unique canonical value in the completed measure, then gives a Borel representative almost everywhere. This alternative proves the measured assertion, not an everywhere Borel selector for arbitrary nonunique relations. The arbitrary standard Borel parameter theorem above cannot be strengthened by intersecting uncountably many parameter-dependent conull sets. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-MSF.md","start_line":106,"end_line":137,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false},"82186463e01706c64c14de2dd51d7057eb4c9ac4800f2612e3a4b1ed15221221":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.covrep.barycentre\"><\/a>\n## Quasi-invariant orthogonal barycentres give normal actions\n\nReturn to the point-norm continuous system $(A,G,\\alpha)$. Suppose the original\nprobability $\\nu$ on $S(A)$ is orthogonal in the precise sense (O3) and is\nquasi-invariant under $T_g\\omega=\\omega\\circ\\alpha_{g^{-1}}$.\nLet $\\mathcal U_g$ be the weighted representation on its canonical integral,\nwhose unitarity, covariance and strong continuity were proved above. The onto\nunitary (O4) defines\n\n$$\\widetilde U_g=V^*\\mathcal U_gV\\quad\\hbox{on }H_\\varphi.\n\\tag{O14}$$\n\nIt is a strongly continuous unitary representation and\n$\\widetilde U_g\\pi_\\varphi(a)\\widetilde U_g^*=\\pi_\\varphi(\\alpha_g(a))$.\nConsequently it normalizes $M=\\pi_\\varphi(A)''$, because unitary conjugation\ncommutes with taking commutants. Its restriction defines normal automorphisms\n$\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}\\widetilde U_g$ of $M$.\n\nThe action is continuous also in the predual sense. A normal functional on $M$\nhas the summable vector-series representation in\n[CP6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6). Pullback under $\\beta_g$ replaces its vectors\nby their $\\widetilde U_g^*$-translates. Each finite initial sum is norm-continuous\nby strong continuity of $\\widetilde U$ and the coefficient norm estimate\n\n$$\\|\\omega_{v,w}-\\omega_{v',w'}\\|\n\\le\\|v-v'\\|\\|w\\|+\\|v'\\|\\|w-w'\\|.$$\n\nCauchy–Schwarz bounds the vector-series tail uniformly in $g$. Approximating\nfirst by a finite sum proves norm continuity of the full predual orbit.\n\nEvery $B\\in VMV^*$ commutes with the scalar diagonal (O12), and hence is\ndecomposable. The [bounded-approximation argument (D19)–(D21)](#oa-flow.covrep.representations),\napplied to this already constructed GNS field, puts its fibres\n$B(\\omega)$ in $\\pi_\\omega(A)''$ almost everywhere. If\n$v(g,\\omega):H_\\omega\\to H_{T_g\\omega}$ are the canonical GNS arrows, then\n\n$$\\bigl(\\operatorname{Ad}\\mathcal U_g(B)\\bigr)(\\omega)\n=v(g,T_g^{-1}\\omega)B(T_g^{-1}\\omega)v(g,T_g^{-1}\\omega)^*.\n\\tag{O15}$$\n\nThe right side is a measurable bounded operator field, by the measurable\noperator-field calculus. Substituting the formulas for $\\mathcal U_g$ and\n$\\mathcal U_{g^{-1}}$ proves (O15) on arbitrary square-integrable sections:\nthe two positive density factors multiply to one by the inverse density law.\nThe equality holds almost everywhere for each fixed $g$ and $B$. It does not\nassert that every measurable field in $\\pi_\\omega(A)''$ belongs to $VMV^*$.\n\nThe averaged equivalent probability used above established strong continuity\nof transport. Orthogonality is required for the original $\\nu$ in (O3);\nthere is no need to assert that the averaged probability is orthogonal.\n\n","html":"<h2>Quasi-invariant orthogonal barycentres give normal actions<\/h2>\n<p>Return to the point-norm continuous system <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((A,G,\\alpha)\\)\">\\((A,G,\\alpha)\\)<\/span>. Suppose the original\nprobability <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S(A)\\)\">\\(S(A)\\)<\/span> is orthogonal in the precise sense (O3) and is\nquasi-invariant under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_g\\omega=\\omega\\circ\\alpha_{g^{-1}}\\)\">\\(T_g\\omega=\\omega\\circ\\alpha_{g^{-1}}\\)<\/span>.\nLet <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal U_g\\)\">\\(\\mathcal U_g\\)<\/span> be the weighted representation on its canonical integral,\nwhose unitarity, covariance and strong continuity were proved above. The onto\nunitary (O4) defines<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\\widetilde U_g=V^*\\mathcal U_gV\\quad\\hbox{on }H_\\varphi.\n\\tag{O14}\\]\">\\[\\widetilde U_g=V^*\\mathcal U_gV\\quad\\hbox{on }H_\\varphi.\n\\tag{O14}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>It is a strongly continuous unitary representation and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde U_g\\pi_\\varphi(a)\\widetilde U_g^*=\\pi_\\varphi(\\alpha_g(a))\\)\">\\(\\widetilde U_g\\pi_\\varphi(a)\\widetilde U_g^*=\\pi_\\varphi(\\alpha_g(a))\\)<\/span>.\nConsequently it normalizes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=\\pi_\\varphi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(M=\\pi_\\varphi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>, because unitary conjugation\ncommutes with taking commutants. Its restriction defines normal automorphisms\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}\\widetilde U_g\\)\">\\(\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}\\widetilde U_g\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The action is continuous also in the predual sense. A normal functional on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>\nhas the summable vector-series representation in\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP6<\/a>. Pullback under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_g\\)\">\\(\\beta_g\\)<\/span> replaces its vectors\nby their <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde U_g^*\\)\">\\(\\widetilde U_g^*\\)<\/span>-translates. Each finite initial sum is norm-continuous\nby strong continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde U\\)\">\\(\\widetilde U\\)<\/span> and the coefficient norm estimate<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\\|\\omega_{v,w}-\\omega_{v&#x27;,w&#x27;}\\|\n\\le\\|v-v&#x27;\\|\\|w\\|+\\|v&#x27;\\|\\|w-w&#x27;\\|.\\]\">\\[\\|\\omega_{v,w}-\\omega_{v&#x27;,w&#x27;}\\|\n\\le\\|v-v&#x27;\\|\\|w\\|+\\|v&#x27;\\|\\|w-w&#x27;\\|.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Cauchy–Schwarz bounds the vector-series tail uniformly in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>. Approximating\nfirst by a finite sum proves norm continuity of the full predual orbit.<\/p>\n<p>Every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\in VMV^*\\)\">\\(B\\in VMV^*\\)<\/span> commutes with the scalar diagonal (O12), and hence is\ndecomposable. The <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-flow.covrep.representations\">bounded-approximation argument (D19)–(D21)<\/a>,\napplied to this already constructed GNS field, puts its fibres\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(\\omega)\\)\">\\(B(\\omega)\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\omega(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\omega(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span> almost everywhere. If\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v(g,\\omega):H_\\omega\\to H_{T_g\\omega}\\)\">\\(v(g,\\omega):H_\\omega\\to H_{T_g\\omega}\\)<\/span> are the canonical GNS arrows, then<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\\bigl(\\operatorname{Ad}\\mathcal U_g(B)\\bigr)(\\omega)\n=v(g,T_g^{-1}\\omega)B(T_g^{-1}\\omega)v(g,T_g^{-1}\\omega)^*.\n\\tag{O15}\\]\">\\[\\bigl(\\operatorname{Ad}\\mathcal U_g(B)\\bigr)(\\omega)\n=v(g,T_g^{-1}\\omega)B(T_g^{-1}\\omega)v(g,T_g^{-1}\\omega)^*.\n\\tag{O15}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The right side is a measurable bounded operator field, by the measurable\noperator-field calculus. Substituting the formulas for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal U_g\\)\">\\(\\mathcal U_g\\)<\/span> and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal U_{g^{-1}}\\)\">\\(\\mathcal U_{g^{-1}}\\)<\/span> proves (O15) on arbitrary square-integrable sections:\nthe two positive density factors multiply to one by the inverse density law.\nThe equality holds almost everywhere for each fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. It does not\nassert that every measurable field in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\omega(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\omega(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span> belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(VMV^*\\)\">\\(VMV^*\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The averaged equivalent probability used above established strong continuity\nof transport. Orthogonality is required for the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> in (O3);\nthere is no need to assert that the averaged probability is orthogonal.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L39.md","start_line":897,"end_line":948,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false},"825d0144bc97604628db97f04eb14efcee2bd4af6e356b50adf2dbfd94aebda1":{"markdown":"<a id=\"sec-4\"><\/a>\n## 4. Convolution stays in the locally Lusin space\n\nFix a faithful normal semifinite weight \\(\\varphi\\) and write the two sections as \\(a(t)\\varphi^{it}\\) and \\(b(t)\\varphi^{it}\\). Abbreviate \\(\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\) to \\(\\sigma_t\\). All operator-valued integrals below are the weak-star integrals of [Section 3](OA-FLOW-SEC.md#sec-3), tested against \\(M_*\\). The coefficient of their proposed convolution is\n\\[\n c(t)=(a*b)(t)\n     =\\int_{\\mathbb R}a(s)\\sigma_s(b(t-s))\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC4.a}\n\\]\nThe modular factor is determined by the [ordered graded product](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-3).\n\nWe use the [faithful normal modular implementation](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.4): represent \\(M\\) faithfully normally on its weight GNS Hilbert space \\(H\\), and write\n\\[\n \\sigma_s(x)=W_sxW_s^*,\n \\qquad W_sW_r=W_{s+r},\n \\tag{SEC4.b}\n\\]\nwhere \\(W\\) is strongly continuous. The dimension of \\(H\\) is unrestricted. This representation preserves the norm and, on bounded sets, identifies the intrinsic sigma-strong-star topology with the concrete strong-star topology by [ST2](OA-FLOW-ST12.md#oa-flow.st.2). The latter agrees there with Mackey topology by [GRD5](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-5).\n\n**Existence at almost every output time.** Fix \\(t\\) and a bounded closed interval \\(I\\). Choose Lusin compact restrictions \\(K_a\\subset I\\) for \\(a\\) and \\(K_b\\subset t-I\\) for \\(b\\). Then\n\\(K=K_a\\cap(t-K_b)\\) is compact, and the measure lost in \\(I\\) is at most the sum of the two losses. On \\(K\\), both coefficient images are norm bounded by [Section 1](OA-FLOW-SEC.md#sec-1). The action is jointly strong-star continuous on bounded sets, either by (SEC4.b) or the [bounded action theorem](OA-FLOW-AT.md#oa-flow.at.4). Bounded products are jointly strong-star continuous as well: if \\(A_i\\to A\\) and \\(B_i\\to B\\) strongly-star, with \\(\\|A_i\\|\\le R\\), then\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|(A_iB_i-AB)\\xi\\|\n &\\le R\\|(B_i-B)\\xi\\|\\\\\n &\\quad+\\|(A_i-A)B\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0,\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC4.c}\n\\]\nand the same estimate for \\(B_i^*A_i^*\\) handles adjoints. Thus\n\\(s\\mapsto a(s)\\sigma_s(b(t-s))\\) is Mackey continuous on \\(K\\). Since the losses can be arbitrarily small, this integrand is locally Lusin.\n\nPut \\(A(s)=\\|a(s)\\|\\), \\(B(s)=\\|b(s)\\|\\). These scalar functions are measurable by Section 1. Its countable compact-cover construction permits Borel representatives after a null modification. The [scalar nonnegative Fubini theorem](OA-FLOW-FF.md#scalar-interchange), with [translation and reflection of Lebesgue measure](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-02), gives\n\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n (A*B)(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}A(s)B(t-s)\\,ds,\\\\\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}(A*B)(t)\\,dt\n       =\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}A\\right)\n          \\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}B\\right)\n       =\\|a\\|_1\\|b\\|_1.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.d}\n\\]\nThese statements also hold for the original completed-measure representatives: on any bounded rectangle, the inverse image of a one-dimensional null set under \\(s\\) or \\(t-s\\) is null by the same scalar theorem.\n\nConsequently\n\\[\n N_{a,b}=\\{t:(A*B)(t)=\\infty\\}\n \\quad\\text{is null}.\n \\tag{SEC4.e}\n\\]\nFor \\(t\\notin N_{a,b}\\), the locally Lusin integrand in (SEC4.a) has integrable norm, so Section 3 defines its integral in \\(M\\), with\n\\(\\|c(t)\\|\\le(A*B)(t)\\). Set \\(c(t)=0\\) on \\(N_{a,b}\\). At this stage this is a pointwise construction and bound; measurability of \\(\\|c(t)\\|\\) will follow from the closure proof below.\n\nChanging \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) on scalar null sets \\(N_a,N_b\\) changes the integrand for a fixed \\(t\\) only on \\(N_a\\cup(t-N_b)\\), which is null. Thus the integral, wherever it exists, is independent of those modifications. All exceptional sets used here come from scalar norm bounds.\n\n**Bounded coefficients with compact support.** First suppose \\(\\|a(s)\\|\\le P\\), \\(\\|b(s)\\|\\le Q\\) for every \\(s\\), and both coefficients vanish outside compact sets. Choose a compact interval \\(I\\) containing the support of \\(a\\). Formula (SEC4.a) then exists for every \\(t\\), and\n\\[\n \\|c(t)\\|\\le Q\\|a\\|_1\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n \\tag{SEC4.f}\n\\]\nWe prove its strong-star continuity at every \\(t\\).\n\nFor \\(\\xi\\in H\\), the vector comparison in Section 3 and (SEC4.b) give\n\\[\n \\|(c(t+h)-c(t))\\xi\\|\n \\le P\\int_I\n    \\|(b(t+h-s)-b(t-s))W_s^*\\xi\\|\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC4.g}\n\\]\nThe continuous path \\(s\\mapsto W_s^*\\xi\\) has compact metric image on \\(I\\). For \\(\\delta>0\\), choose a finite-valued measurable function\n\\(\\eta_\\delta(s)=\\sum_{j=1}^m1_{E_j}(s)\\eta_j\\) with uniform error at most \\(\\delta\\). Such a function is obtained from a finite \\(\\delta\\)-net of the compact image by assigning the first suitable net point; the resulting sets \\(E_j\\) are measurable. Replacing \\(W_s^*\\xi\\) by \\(\\eta_\\delta(s)\\) in (SEC4.g) costs at most\n\\[\n 2PQ|I|\\delta.\n \\tag{SEC4.h}\n\\]\nFor each of its finitely many values, the remaining term is bounded by\n\\[\n P\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n       \\|(b(r+h)-b(r))\\eta_j\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC4.i}\n\\]\nIndeed \\(r=t-s\\) changes the integral over \\(E_j\\) to one over a measurable subset of \\(\\mathbb R\\), and we enlarge it to the whole line. The vector function \\(r\\mapsto b(r)\\eta_j\\) is strongly measurable and integrable on the arbitrary space \\(H\\), by Section 3. Its integral-norm translation continuity is proved there from [scalar translations](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.2) and [vector simple approximation](OA-FLOW-L24.md#oa-flow.grp.vectorintegration). Let \\(h\\to0\\), then \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\). This proves strong continuity.\n\nThe adjoints require a separate estimate. Commuting the adjoint with the weak-star integral as in Section 3 gives\n\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n \\|(c(t+h)^*-c(t)^*)\\xi\\|\\\\\n \\le\\int_I\n     \\|(b(t+h-s)^*-b(t-s)^*)\\zeta(s)\\|\\,ds,\\\\\n \\zeta(s)=W_s^*a(s)^*\\xi.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.j}\n\\]\nThe function \\(\\zeta\\) is strongly measurable on \\(I\\): on each of the countably many Lusin compact restrictions of \\(a\\), it is a continuous \\(H\\)-valued function with compact metric image. These images lie in a separable closed subspace after taking their countable union, as proved in Section 3; no separability of \\(H\\) is required. Also \\(\\|\\zeta(s)\\|\\le P\\|\\xi\\|\\), so \\(\\zeta\\in L^1(I,H)\\).\n\nApproximate \\(\\zeta\\) in \\(L^1(I,H)\\) by a finite-valued measurable function \\(\\sum_j1_{E_j}\\zeta_j\\). The error in (SEC4.j) is at most\n\\(2Q\\|\\zeta-\\sum_j1_{E_j}\\zeta_j\\|_{L^1(I,H)}\\).\nFor each \\(\\zeta_j\\), the remaining integral is bounded by\n\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\n       \\|(b(r+h)^*-b(r)^*)\\zeta_j\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0,\n \\tag{SEC4.k}\n\\]\nusing translation continuity of \\(r\\mapsto b(r)^*\\zeta_j\\). First take \\(h\\to0\\), then make the approximation error arbitrarily small. This proves adjoint continuity. The global norm bound (SEC4.f) now makes \\(c\\) Mackey continuous by GRD5. Thus the convolution of these bounded compactly supported coefficients is locally Lusin, with no assertion that either input is norm measurable as an \\(M\\)-valued function.\n\n**General coefficients.** Take the bounded compactly supported truncations \\(a_n,b_n\\) from [Section 1](OA-FLOW-SEC.md#sec-1), and put \\(c_n=a_n*b_n\\). Each \\(c_n\\) is everywhere Mackey continuous by the preceding argument. Let \\(c\\) be the pointwise weak-star convolution already constructed. For \\(t\\notin N_{a,b}\\), subtract the two integrals and split the difference to obtain\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|c(t)-c_n(t)\\|&\\le d_n(t),\\\\\n d_n&=\\|a-a_n\\|*\\|b\\|\n           +\\|a_n\\|*\\|b-b_n\\|.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC4.l}\n\\]\nAll four functions on the right are already measurable scalar functions. In particular no measurability of the expression on the left has been used. Scalar Tonelli gives\n\\[\n \\int d_n\n =\\|a-a_n\\|_1\\|b\\|_1\n    +\\|a_n\\|_1\\|b-b_n\\|_1\n \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC4.m}\n\\]\nThe truncations are scalar masks, so their norms and the difference norms are bounded pointwise by \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), respectively. Hence every integral used in (SEC4.l) is finite outside the same \\(N_{a,b}\\).\n\nChoose a subsequence \\(n_k\\) with \\(\\sum_k\\int d_{n_k}<\\infty\\), and form the measurable scalar tails\n\\[\n D_j(t)=\\sum_{k\\ge j}d_{n_k}(t).\n \\tag{SEC4.n}\n\\]\nThe [nonnegative sum and convergence theorem](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-04) shows that \\(D_j\\downarrow0\\) outside a scalar null set and that \\(\\int D_j\\to0\\). On any bounded closed interval \\(J\\), the uniform-tail argument of [Section 2](OA-FLOW-SEC.md#sec-2) applies to these scalar functions. More explicitly, choose increasing indices \\(j_m\\) with\n\\[\n |\\{t\\in J:D_{j_m}(t)>1/m\\}|<\\varepsilon2^{-m-3}.\n \\tag{SEC4.o}\n\\]\nOutside the union of these sets, \\(D_j\\to0\\) uniformly. Remove also a small open neighborhood of \\(N_{a,b}\\) and of the null set where the tails fail. [Lebesgue regularity](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.2) gives a compact subset \\(K\\subset J\\) of the remaining set with \\(|J\\setminus K|<\\varepsilon\\). On \\(K\\), (SEC4.l) and \\(d_{n_k}\\le D_k\\) prove uniform convergence \\(c_{n_k}\\to c\\) in operator norm.\n\nFor a weakly compact predual set \\(L\\), its norm bound gives\n\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n p_L(c_{n_k}(t)-c(t))\n \\le\\left(\\sup_{\\omega\\in L}\\|\\omega\\|\\right)d_{n_k}(t),\\\\\n \\sup_{t\\in K}d_{n_k}(t)\\longrightarrow0.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.p}\n\\]\nThus \\(c|_K\\) is a uniform limit for every Mackey seminorm of continuous maps and is Mackey continuous. This proves the required local Lusin property of \\(c\\). Only now use Section 1 to conclude measurability of its norm. The pointwise scalar bound and (SEC4.d) yield Young's inequality\n\\[\n \\boxed{\\ \\|a*b\\|_1\\le\\|a\\|_1\\|b\\|_1.\\ }\n \\tag{SEC4.q}\n\\]\nConvolution is bilinear on almost-everywhere classes, by linearity of the weak-star integral off the finite union of the relevant scalar exceptional sets. In particular it is a bounded bilinear operation on the Banach space of Section 2.\n\n","html":"<h2>4. Convolution stays in the locally Lusin space<\/h2>\n<p>Fix a faithful normal semifinite weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> and write the two sections as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a(t)\\varphi^{it}\\)\">\\(a(t)\\varphi^{it}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b(t)\\varphi^{it}\\)\">\\(b(t)\\varphi^{it}\\)<\/span>. Abbreviate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t\\)<\/span>. All operator-valued integrals below are the weak-star integrals of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SEC.html#sec-3\">Section 3<\/a>, tested against <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span>. The coefficient of their proposed convolution is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c(t)=(a*b)(t)\n     =\\int_{\\mathbb R}a(s)\\sigma_s(b(t-s))\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC4.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n c(t)=(a*b)(t)\n     =\\int_{\\mathbb R}a(s)\\sigma_s(b(t-s))\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC4.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe modular factor is determined by the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-3\">ordered graded product<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>We use the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.4\">faithful normal modular implementation<\/a>: represent <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> faithfully normally on its weight GNS Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, and write\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sigma_s(x)=W_sxW_s^*,\n \\qquad W_sW_r=W_{s+r},\n \\tag{SEC4.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sigma_s(x)=W_sxW_s^*,\n \\qquad W_sW_r=W_{s+r},\n \\tag{SEC4.b}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is strongly continuous. The dimension of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is unrestricted. This representation preserves the norm and, on bounded sets, identifies the intrinsic sigma-strong-star topology with the concrete strong-star topology by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-ST12.html#oa-flow.st.2\">ST2<\/a>. The latter agrees there with Mackey topology by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-5\">GRD5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Existence at almost every output time.<\/strong> Fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> and a bounded closed interval <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>. Choose Lusin compact restrictions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_a\\subset I\\)\">\\(K_a\\subset I\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_b\\subset t-I\\)\">\\(K_b\\subset t-I\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>. Then\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=K_a\\cap(t-K_b)\\)\">\\(K=K_a\\cap(t-K_b)\\)<\/span> is compact, and the measure lost in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is at most the sum of the two losses. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, both coefficient images are norm bounded by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SEC.html#sec-1\">Section 1<\/a>. The action is jointly strong-star continuous on bounded sets, either by (SEC4.b) or the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-AT.html#oa-flow.at.4\">bounded action theorem<\/a>. Bounded products are jointly strong-star continuous as well: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_i\\to A\\)\">\\(A_i\\to A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_i\\to B\\)\">\\(B_i\\to B\\)<\/span> strongly-star, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|A_i\\|\\le R\\)\">\\(\\|A_i\\|\\le R\\)<\/span>, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|(A_iB_i-AB)\\xi\\|\n &amp;\\le R\\|(B_i-B)\\xi\\|\\\\\n &amp;\\quad+\\|(A_i-A)B\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0,\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC4.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|(A_iB_i-AB)\\xi\\|\n &amp;\\le R\\|(B_i-B)\\xi\\|\\\\\n &amp;\\quad+\\|(A_i-A)B\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0,\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC4.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nand the same estimate for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_i^*A_i^*\\)\">\\(B_i^*A_i^*\\)<\/span> handles adjoints. Thus\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\mapsto a(s)\\sigma_s(b(t-s))\\)\">\\(s\\mapsto a(s)\\sigma_s(b(t-s))\\)<\/span> is Mackey continuous on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>. Since the losses can be arbitrarily small, this integrand is locally Lusin.<\/p>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A(s)=\\|a(s)\\|\\)\">\\(A(s)=\\|a(s)\\|\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(s)=\\|b(s)\\|\\)\">\\(B(s)=\\|b(s)\\|\\)<\/span>. These scalar functions are measurable by Section 1. Its countable compact-cover construction permits Borel representatives after a null modification. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#scalar-interchange\">scalar nonnegative Fubini theorem<\/a>, with <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-02\">translation and reflection of Lebesgue measure<\/a>, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n (A*B)(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}A(s)B(t-s)\\,ds,\\\\\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}(A*B)(t)\\,dt\n       =\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}A\\right)\n          \\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}B\\right)\n       =\\|a\\|_1\\|b\\|_1.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.d}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n (A*B)(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}A(s)B(t-s)\\,ds,\\\\\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}(A*B)(t)\\,dt\n       =\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}A\\right)\n          \\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}B\\right)\n       =\\|a\\|_1\\|b\\|_1.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.d}\n\\]<\/span>\nThese statements also hold for the original completed-measure representatives: on any bounded rectangle, the inverse image of a one-dimensional null set under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t-s\\)\">\\(t-s\\)<\/span> is null by the same scalar theorem.<\/p>\n<p>Consequently\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n N_{a,b}=\\{t:(A*B)(t)=\\infty\\}\n \\quad\\text{is null}.\n \\tag{SEC4.e}\n\\]\">\\[\n N_{a,b}=\\{t:(A*B)(t)=\\infty\\}\n \\quad\\text{is null}.\n \\tag{SEC4.e}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\notin N_{a,b}\\)\">\\(t\\notin N_{a,b}\\)<\/span>, the locally Lusin integrand in (SEC4.a) has integrable norm, so Section 3 defines its integral in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|c(t)\\|\\le(A*B)(t)\\)\">\\(\\|c(t)\\|\\le(A*B)(t)\\)<\/span>. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c(t)=0\\)\">\\(c(t)=0\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_{a,b}\\)\">\\(N_{a,b}\\)<\/span>. At this stage this is a pointwise construction and bound; measurability of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|c(t)\\|\\)\">\\(\\|c(t)\\|\\)<\/span> will follow from the closure proof below.<\/p>\n<p>Changing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> on scalar null sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_a,N_b\\)\">\\(N_a,N_b\\)<\/span> changes the integrand for a fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> only on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_a\\cup(t-N_b)\\)\">\\(N_a\\cup(t-N_b)\\)<\/span>, which is null. Thus the integral, wherever it exists, is independent of those modifications. All exceptional sets used here come from scalar norm bounds.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Bounded coefficients with compact support.<\/strong> First suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|a(s)\\|\\le P\\)\">\\(\\|a(s)\\|\\le P\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|b(s)\\|\\le Q\\)\">\\(\\|b(s)\\|\\le Q\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>, and both coefficients vanish outside compact sets. Choose a compact interval <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> containing the support of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>. Formula (SEC4.a) then exists for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|c(t)\\|\\le Q\\|a\\|_1\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n \\tag{SEC4.f}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|c(t)\\|\\le Q\\|a\\|_1\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n \\tag{SEC4.f}\n\\]<\/span>\nWe prove its strong-star continuity at every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in H\\)<\/span>, the vector comparison in Section 3 and (SEC4.b) give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|(c(t+h)-c(t))\\xi\\|\n \\le P\\int_I\n    \\|(b(t+h-s)-b(t-s))W_s^*\\xi\\|\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC4.g}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|(c(t+h)-c(t))\\xi\\|\n \\le P\\int_I\n    \\|(b(t+h-s)-b(t-s))W_s^*\\xi\\|\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC4.g}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe continuous path <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\mapsto W_s^*\\xi\\)\">\\(s\\mapsto W_s^*\\xi\\)<\/span> has compact metric image on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)<\/span>, choose a finite-valued measurable function\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_\\delta(s)=\\sum_{j=1}^m1_{E_j}(s)\\eta_j\\)\">\\(\\eta_\\delta(s)=\\sum_{j=1}^m1_{E_j}(s)\\eta_j\\)<\/span> with uniform error at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\)\">\\(\\delta\\)<\/span>. Such a function is obtained from a finite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\)\">\\(\\delta\\)<\/span>-net of the compact image by assigning the first suitable net point; the resulting sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_j\\)\">\\(E_j\\)<\/span> are measurable. Replacing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_s^*\\xi\\)\">\\(W_s^*\\xi\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_\\delta(s)\\)\">\\(\\eta_\\delta(s)\\)<\/span> in (SEC4.g) costs at most\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n 2PQ|I|\\delta.\n \\tag{SEC4.h}\n\\]\">\\[\n 2PQ|I|\\delta.\n \\tag{SEC4.h}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor each of its finitely many values, the remaining term is bounded by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n P\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n       \\|(b(r+h)-b(r))\\eta_j\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC4.i}\n\\]\">\\[\n P\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n       \\|(b(r+h)-b(r))\\eta_j\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC4.i}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=t-s\\)\">\\(r=t-s\\)<\/span> changes the integral over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_j\\)\">\\(E_j\\)<\/span> to one over a measurable subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, and we enlarge it to the whole line. The vector function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\mapsto b(r)\\eta_j\\)\">\\(r\\mapsto b(r)\\eta_j\\)<\/span> is strongly measurable and integrable on the arbitrary space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, by Section 3. Its integral-norm translation continuity is proved there from <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.2\">scalar translations<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L24.html#oa-flow.grp.vectorintegration\">vector simple approximation<\/a>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\to0\\)\">\\(h\\to0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\)\">\\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\)<\/span>. This proves strong continuity.<\/p>\n<p>The adjoints require a separate estimate. Commuting the adjoint with the weak-star integral as in Section 3 gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n \\|(c(t+h)^*-c(t)^*)\\xi\\|\\\\\n \\le\\int_I\n     \\|(b(t+h-s)^*-b(t-s)^*)\\zeta(s)\\|\\,ds,\\\\\n \\zeta(s)=W_s^*a(s)^*\\xi.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.j}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n \\|(c(t+h)^*-c(t)^*)\\xi\\|\\\\\n \\le\\int_I\n     \\|(b(t+h-s)^*-b(t-s)^*)\\zeta(s)\\|\\,ds,\\\\\n \\zeta(s)=W_s^*a(s)^*\\xi.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.j}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\)<\/span> is strongly measurable on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>: on each of the countably many Lusin compact restrictions of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>, it is a continuous <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>-valued function with compact metric image. These images lie in a separable closed subspace after taking their countable union, as proved in Section 3; no separability of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is required. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\zeta(s)\\|\\le P\\|\\xi\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\zeta(s)\\|\\le P\\|\\xi\\|\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\in L^1(I,H)\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\in L^1(I,H)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Approximate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1(I,H)\\)\">\\(L^1(I,H)\\)<\/span> by a finite-valued measurable function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_j1_{E_j}\\zeta_j\\)\">\\(\\sum_j1_{E_j}\\zeta_j\\)<\/span>. The error in (SEC4.j) is at most\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2Q\\|\\zeta-\\sum_j1_{E_j}\\zeta_j\\|_{L^1(I,H)}\\)\">\\(2Q\\|\\zeta-\\sum_j1_{E_j}\\zeta_j\\|_{L^1(I,H)}\\)<\/span>.\nFor each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta_j\\)\">\\(\\zeta_j\\)<\/span>, the remaining integral is bounded by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\n       \\|(b(r+h)^*-b(r)^*)\\zeta_j\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0,\n \\tag{SEC4.k}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\n       \\|(b(r+h)^*-b(r)^*)\\zeta_j\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0,\n \\tag{SEC4.k}\n\\]<\/span>\nusing translation continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\mapsto b(r)^*\\zeta_j\\)\">\\(r\\mapsto b(r)^*\\zeta_j\\)<\/span>. First take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\to0\\)\">\\(h\\to0\\)<\/span>, then make the approximation error arbitrarily small. This proves adjoint continuity. The global norm bound (SEC4.f) now makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span> Mackey continuous by GRD5. Thus the convolution of these bounded compactly supported coefficients is locally Lusin, with no assertion that either input is norm measurable as an <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>-valued function.<\/p>\n<p><strong>General coefficients.<\/strong> Take the bounded compactly supported truncations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_n,b_n\\)\">\\(a_n,b_n\\)<\/span> from <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SEC.html#sec-1\">Section 1<\/a>, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_n=a_n*b_n\\)\">\\(c_n=a_n*b_n\\)<\/span>. Each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_n\\)\">\\(c_n\\)<\/span> is everywhere Mackey continuous by the preceding argument. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span> be the pointwise weak-star convolution already constructed. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\notin N_{a,b}\\)\">\\(t\\notin N_{a,b}\\)<\/span>, subtract the two integrals and split the difference to obtain\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|c(t)-c_n(t)\\|&amp;\\le d_n(t),\\\\\n d_n&amp;=\\|a-a_n\\|*\\|b\\|\n           +\\|a_n\\|*\\|b-b_n\\|.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC4.l}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|c(t)-c_n(t)\\|&amp;\\le d_n(t),\\\\\n d_n&amp;=\\|a-a_n\\|*\\|b\\|\n           +\\|a_n\\|*\\|b-b_n\\|.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC4.l}\n\\]<\/span>\nAll four functions on the right are already measurable scalar functions. In particular no measurability of the expression on the left has been used. Scalar Tonelli gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int d_n\n =\\|a-a_n\\|_1\\|b\\|_1\n    +\\|a_n\\|_1\\|b-b_n\\|_1\n \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC4.m}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int d_n\n =\\|a-a_n\\|_1\\|b\\|_1\n    +\\|a_n\\|_1\\|b-b_n\\|_1\n \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC4.m}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe truncations are scalar masks, so their norms and the difference norms are bounded pointwise by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, respectively. Hence every integral used in (SEC4.l) is finite outside the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_{a,b}\\)\">\\(N_{a,b}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a subsequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n_k\\)\">\\(n_k\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_k\\int d_{n_k}&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_k\\int d_{n_k}&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>, and form the measurable scalar tails\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n D_j(t)=\\sum_{k\\ge j}d_{n_k}(t).\n \\tag{SEC4.n}\n\\]\">\\[\n D_j(t)=\\sum_{k\\ge j}d_{n_k}(t).\n \\tag{SEC4.n}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-04\">nonnegative sum and convergence theorem<\/a> shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_j\\downarrow0\\)\">\\(D_j\\downarrow0\\)<\/span> outside a scalar null set and that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int D_j\\to0\\)\">\\(\\int D_j\\to0\\)<\/span>. On any bounded closed interval <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span>, the uniform-tail argument of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SEC.html#sec-2\">Section 2<\/a> applies to these scalar functions. More explicitly, choose increasing indices <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j_m\\)\">\\(j_m\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n |\\{t\\in J:D_{j_m}(t)&gt;1/m\\}|&lt;\\varepsilon2^{-m-3}.\n \\tag{SEC4.o}\n\\]\">\\[\n |\\{t\\in J:D_{j_m}(t)&gt;1/m\\}|&lt;\\varepsilon2^{-m-3}.\n \\tag{SEC4.o}\n\\]<\/span>\nOutside the union of these sets, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_j\\to0\\)\">\\(D_j\\to0\\)<\/span> uniformly. Remove also a small open neighborhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_{a,b}\\)\">\\(N_{a,b}\\)<\/span> and of the null set where the tails fail. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.2\">Lebesgue regularity<\/a> gives a compact subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\subset J\\)\">\\(K\\subset J\\)<\/span> of the remaining set with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|J\\setminus K|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)\">\\(|J\\setminus K|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)<\/span>. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, (SEC4.l) and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_{n_k}\\le D_k\\)\">\\(d_{n_k}\\le D_k\\)<\/span> prove uniform convergence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_{n_k}\\to c\\)\">\\(c_{n_k}\\to c\\)<\/span> in operator norm.<\/p>\n<p>For a weakly compact predual set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>, its norm bound gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n p_L(c_{n_k}(t)-c(t))\n \\le\\left(\\sup_{\\omega\\in L}\\|\\omega\\|\\right)d_{n_k}(t),\\\\\n \\sup_{t\\in K}d_{n_k}(t)\\longrightarrow0.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.p}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n p_L(c_{n_k}(t)-c(t))\n \\le\\left(\\sup_{\\omega\\in L}\\|\\omega\\|\\right)d_{n_k}(t),\\\\\n \\sup_{t\\in K}d_{n_k}(t)\\longrightarrow0.\n \\end{gathered}\n \\tag{SEC4.p}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c|_K\\)\">\\(c|_K\\)<\/span> is a uniform limit for every Mackey seminorm of continuous maps and is Mackey continuous. This proves the required local Lusin property of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span>. Only now use Section 1 to conclude measurability of its norm. The pointwise scalar bound and (SEC4.d) yield Young's inequality\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\boxed{\\ \\|a*b\\|_1\\le\\|a\\|_1\\|b\\|_1.\\ }\n \\tag{SEC4.q}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\boxed{\\ \\|a*b\\|_1\\le\\|a\\|_1\\|b\\|_1.\\ }\n \\tag{SEC4.q}\n\\]<\/span>\nConvolution is bilinear on almost-everywhere classes, by linearity of the weak-star integral off the finite union of the relevant scalar exceptional sets. In particular it is a bounded bilinear operation on the Banach space of Section 2.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-SEC.md","start_line":303,"end_line":455,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false}});
